Exactly.

255
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:33,840
And examiners will definitely test that relationship.

256
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:39,680
The deep one, so the profundus, passes through the superficial one, the superficialis, not

257
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:40,680
the other way around.

258
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:41,680
Okay.

259
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:43,280
So let's summarize these upper limb traps before we move on.

260
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:47,720
So mentally bullet-pointing this, we track the exceptions, right?

261
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:51,760
The brachialis has that dual innervation because of migration.

262
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:52,760
Right.

263
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:57,320
The trapezius long head is inside the capsule, but wrapped in its own synovial wallpaper.

264
00:10:57,320 --> 00:10:59,520
So intercapsular, but extra synovial.

265
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:04,920
The deep finger flexor, the profundus, threads the needle right through the superficial one.

266
00:11:04,920 --> 00:11:09,920
And the medial fascial septum is the thick, strong one, and it's pierced by the ulnar

267
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:11,880
nerve going front to back.

268
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:12,880
Perfect summary.

269
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:17,260
And, you know, keep that dual innervation concept in your mind because as we transition

270
00:11:17,260 --> 00:11:21,240
away from the highly mobile arms and we look at the lower limbs, we are going to see that

271
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:26,000
exact same pattern of evolutionary migration, just on a much larger scale.

272
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Right.

273
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,320
Because the lower limb, well, it isn't really about precision.

274
00:11:29,320 --> 00:11:32,360
It's about massive load-bearing stability and locomotion.

275
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:33,360
Right.

276
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:36,620
I mean, we are dealing with massive chunks of muscle here.

277
00:11:36,620 --> 00:11:39,240
Do they really migrate and break the rules too?

278
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:40,240
They absolutely do.

279
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:44,320
But in the lower limb, because the muscles are just so large, the exam questions usually

280
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:47,920
revolve around exactly which joints these massive cables cross.

281
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:48,920
Okay.

282
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:51,520
The rectus femoris sits on the anterior thigh, the quadriceps femoris.

283
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:54,600
So four muscles acting as the primary extensor of the knee.

284
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:55,600
Right.

285
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:56,600
And three of them are called the vasti muscles.

286
00:11:56,600 --> 00:12:01,520
So you have the vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, and vastus intermedius.

287
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:04,600
All three of these originate directly off the femur bone.

288
00:12:04,600 --> 00:12:08,880
They travel down, they cross the knee joint, and they attach to your shin to straighten

289
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:09,880
your leg.

290
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:10,880
Very straightforward.

291
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:13,280
One bone to another, crossing just one joint.

292
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:17,800
But the fourth muscle, the rectus femoris, sits right on top of them and totally breaks

293
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:18,800
the pattern.

294
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:19,800
Oh, how so?

295
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:21,160
Well, it doesn't originate on the femur.

296
00:12:21,160 --> 00:12:26,560
It actually reaches all the way up and originates from the anterior inferior iliac spine of

297
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:27,560
the pelvis.

298
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:28,560
Wait.

299
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:29,560
So it starts above the hip joint.

300
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:31,920
Which means it crosses the hip and the knee.

301
00:12:31,920 --> 00:12:32,920
Exactly.

302
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:35,720
The vasti muscles can only extend the knee.

303
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:39,520
But because the rectus femoris crosses the hip, it can also flex the thigh.

304
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:41,160
You know, like when you lift your knee to march?

305
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:42,160
Oh, I see.

306
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:46,440
So all four share the femoral nerve, but only one crosses two joints.

307
00:12:46,440 --> 00:12:48,040
And that is the trap.

308
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:52,040
Doctors will write, you know, all muscles of the quadriceps femoris originate from the

309
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:53,040
femur.

310
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:54,240
And it's false.

311
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:57,000
Because the rectus femoris is anchored to the pelvis.

312
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Got it.

313
00:12:58,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Okay.

314
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:00,000
What about the medial thigh?

315
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:01,000
No.

316
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:02,000
The adductors?

317
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,440
So the adductor compartment is meant to pull your leg inward toward the midline.

318
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:12,280
You have the pectineus, adductor brevis, adductor longus, adductor magnus, and gracilis.

319
00:13:12,280 --> 00:13:16,320
And the rule here is that they are all innervated by the obturator nerve.

320
00:13:16,320 --> 00:13:18,240
But I'm looking for the rebel, right?

321
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:20,920
Which one has an extension cord from another neighborhood?

322
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:22,540
That would be the adductor magnus.

323
00:13:22,540 --> 00:13:25,240
It is the largest of the group, and it is a true hybrid.

324
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:29,800
It originates from both the pubis and the ischium of the pelvis.

325
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:34,160
And functionally, part of it acts like a regular adductor, but the back part of it actually

326
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:35,680
acts like a hamstring muscle.

327
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:36,680
So let me guess.

328
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:37,680
Dual innervation.

329
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:38,680
Yes.

330
00:13:38,680 --> 00:13:42,720
The adductor part gets the obturator nerve, but the hamstring part gets the sciatic nerve.

331
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:44,840
It literally plays for two different teams.

332
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:47,560
That is a direct parallel to the brachialis in the arm.

333
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:50,800
I mean, both of them are these dual innervated anomalies.

334
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:53,680
That makes it a perfect accept question.

335
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:59,040
Like, which medial thigh muscle is not solely innervated by the obturator nerve, the adductor

336
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:00,040
magnus?

337
00:14:00,040 --> 00:14:01,040
Exactly.

338
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:03,040
Now let's move to the back of the thigh, the hamstrings.

339
00:14:03,040 --> 00:14:07,800
So you have the biceps, femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus.

340
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:08,800
Right.

341
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:13,280
The general rule is that they all originate from the ischial tuberosity, which is, that's

342
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:16,480
the hard bone in your pelvis you feel when you sit on a hard chair.

343
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:18,320
And they run down to flex the knee.

344
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:20,640
But biceps implies two heads.

345
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:22,400
Does one of the heads break the rule?

346
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:23,720
Yes, it does.

347
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:28,280
The long head of the biceps femoris attaches to the ischial tuberosity, so it crosses the

348
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:29,280
hip.

349
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:32,880
But the short head originates halfway down the thigh, right off the femur.

350
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:35,640
Ah, so it doesn't cross the hip joint at all.

351
00:14:35,640 --> 00:14:38,800
Just like the vasti muscles in the front, it only acts on the knee.

352
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:40,760
I am seeing the pattern here.

353
00:14:40,760 --> 00:14:45,160
Muscle writers love testing origins to see if you realize a specific muscle head falls

354
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:46,240
short of a joint.

355
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:47,240
They really do.

356
00:14:47,240 --> 00:14:48,600
Let's look up at the glutes.

357
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:51,480
So the gluteus maximus is obviously the powerhouse.

358
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:52,480
It is massive.

359
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:58,600
It originates from the ilium, sacrum, and cossachex, and it extends the thigh.

360
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:03,520
It's what powers you when you're climbing stairs or standing up from a deep squat.

361
00:15:03,520 --> 00:15:06,240
But there are two smaller muscles underneath it, right?

362
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:08,440
The gluteus medius minimus.

363
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:13,400
Now, my intuition tells me that because they are in the exact same area, they probably

364
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:16,640
just do a weaker version of what the maximus does.

365
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:20,280
And that intuition right there is exactly what the examiner relies on to trick you.

366
00:15:20,280 --> 00:15:21,280
Oh, no!

367
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:23,480
Yeah, the medius and minimus do not extend the thigh.

368
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:27,240
Their primary job is to abduct the thigh, so pull it out to the side.

369
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:29,480
But their secondary action is the trap.

370
00:15:29,480 --> 00:15:31,560
They medially rotate the thigh.

371
00:15:31,560 --> 00:15:34,800
Wait, medially rotate, like they twist your leg inward.

372
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:37,560
That feels completely wrong.

373
00:15:37,560 --> 00:15:39,840
Because they're located on the outside of your hip.

374
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:44,660
If you pull from the outside, shouldn't the leg flare outward in lateral rotation?

375
00:15:44,660 --> 00:15:48,040
It feels wrong until you look at the actual physics of the hip joint.

376
00:15:48,040 --> 00:15:49,280
Okay, explain that.

377
00:15:49,280 --> 00:15:53,280
So the axis of rotation for your femur runs right down the middle of the bone.

378
00:15:53,280 --> 00:15:57,600
But the gluteus medius and minimus attach to the greater trochanter, which is a bony

379
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:00,120
projection that sits way out on the lateral side.

380
00:16:00,120 --> 00:16:01,880
Okay, I'm visualizing it.

381
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:06,460
Furthermore, the interior fibers of these muscles actually wrap around to the front

382
00:16:06,460 --> 00:16:07,760
of that trochanter.

383
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:12,140
So when those muscles contract, they yank the front of the trochanter up toward the

384
00:16:12,140 --> 00:16:13,140
ilium.

385
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:18,060
And because of the leverage, that forward pull causes the entire shaft of the femur to

386
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:19,500
pivot inward.

387
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:22,780
Oh, I get it.

388
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:25,300
It's like pushing on the outside edge of a revolving door.

389
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:29,100
You're pushing on the outside, but it causes the whole mechanism to rotate inward around

390
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:30,380
the center inch.

391
00:16:30,380 --> 00:16:32,660
That is a phenomenal analogy, yes.

392
00:16:32,660 --> 00:16:36,540
Because of that mechanical leverage, they are powerful medial rotators.

393
00:16:36,540 --> 00:16:40,940
And test writers will constantly claim they are lateral rotators because they know students

394
00:16:40,940 --> 00:16:44,140
will just rely on bad intuition instead of the physics.

395
00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:45,140
Wow.

396
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:49,020
So true or false, gluteus medius acts to laterally rotate the thigh.

397
00:16:49,020 --> 00:16:50,020
False.

398
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:51,020
It twists it inward.

399
00:16:51,020 --> 00:16:52,020
Exactly.

400
00:16:52,020 --> 00:16:53,020
Okay.

401
00:16:53,020 --> 00:16:54,020
One more lower limb trap.

402
00:16:54,020 --> 00:16:55,340
Let's look at the calf muscles, the posterior lower leg.

403
00:16:55,340 --> 00:16:56,340
Right.

404
00:16:56,340 --> 00:17:00,760
So we call it the triceps surae, consisting of the gastrocnemius, which is the visible

405
00:17:00,840 --> 00:17:05,640
bulging muscle on your calf, and the soleus, the flat muscle sitting deep to it.

406
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:11,280
Both of their tendons merge to form the Achilles tendon, which inserts into the calcaneus,

407
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:12,720
your heel bone.

408
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:16,600
And they both point your toes, what we call plantar flexion.

409
00:17:16,600 --> 00:17:20,160
So they share an insertion and they share an action.

410
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:22,800
Let me guess, they differ at their origin.

411
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:24,560
One crosses the knee and one doesn't.

412
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:26,320
You're reading The Matrix now.

413
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:31,480
Yeah, the gastrocnemius originates from the medial and lateral condyles of the femur.

414
00:17:31,480 --> 00:17:35,120
So it physically crosses the back of the knee joint.

415
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:39,960
But the soleus originates purely from the tibia and fibula in the lower leg.

416
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:41,400
It never reaches the knee.

417
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:44,760
How does that actually play out in real life, functionally?

418
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:46,080
Think about being at the gym, right?

419
00:17:46,080 --> 00:17:49,720
If you do standing calf raises, your knees are locked straight.

420
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:53,480
This stretches the gastrocnemius tight across the back of the knee, allowing it to generate

421
00:17:53,480 --> 00:17:56,160
maximum force to lift your heels.

422
00:17:56,160 --> 00:18:01,320
But if you sit down at a seated calf raise machine, your knees are bent at 90 degrees.

423
00:18:01,320 --> 00:18:05,240
And if the knee is bent, the origin and insertion of the gastrocnemius are brought closer together

424
00:18:05,240 --> 00:18:06,240
so it goes slack.

425
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:07,240
Exactly.

426
00:18:07,240 --> 00:18:09,400
It becomes mechanically disadvantaged.

427
00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:13,720
So when you do a seated calf raise, the soleus, which doesn't care about the knee angle because

428
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:17,040
it doesn't cross the joint, has to do almost all the work.

429
00:18:17,040 --> 00:18:18,040
That is brilliant.

430
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:19,480
Functional anatomy at its finest.

431
00:18:19,480 --> 00:18:24,200
So running down our lower limb summary for the exam, we track the joint crossers, rectus

432
00:18:24,200 --> 00:18:26,080
femoris crosses the hip.

433
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:28,120
Gastrocnemius crosses the knee.

434
00:18:28,120 --> 00:18:31,600
Memorize the rebels, like the dual innervated endoductor magnus.

435
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:34,760
And trust the physics, not your intuition.

436
00:18:34,760 --> 00:18:38,680
So gluteus medius and minimus medially rotate the thigh.

437
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:39,680
Spot on.

438
00:18:39,680 --> 00:18:40,880
So let's shift gears and look at the trunk.

439
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:45,680
We are moving from the chaotic migration of the limbs back into the core.

440
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:49,960
You know, when I look at a textbook diagram of the back muscles, it looks like an absolute

441
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:50,960
disaster.

442
00:18:50,960 --> 00:18:56,040
I mean, it's just a solid wall of meat with fibers overlapping in 50 different directions.

443
00:18:56,040 --> 00:18:59,880
It's incredibly intimidating compared to the clean compartments of the arm.

444
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:02,560
Is it completely chaotic or is there an underlying order to it?

445
00:19:02,560 --> 00:19:08,160
It definitely looks chaotic on the surface, but the back is governed by a profoundly strict,

446
00:19:08,160 --> 00:19:10,280
highly tested paradigm.

447
00:19:10,280 --> 00:19:15,200
The key to the back isn't memorizing 50 muscle names in a vacuum.

448
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:20,040
It's understanding that there is this deep embryological divide based entirely on nerve

449
00:19:20,040 --> 00:19:21,040
supply.

450
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:25,220
OK, so if I pull a muscle in my back while I'm deadlifting, how do I know if I hurt

451
00:19:25,220 --> 00:19:29,820
a true back muscle or if you just hurt an arm muscle that happens to be anchored on

452
00:19:29,820 --> 00:19:30,820
my back?

453
00:19:30,820 --> 00:19:32,700
That is exactly the distinction you need to make.

454
00:19:32,700 --> 00:19:37,780
The muscles on your back are strictly classified into two massive categories, extrinsic and

455
00:19:37,780 --> 00:19:39,100
intrinsic.

456
00:19:39,100 --> 00:19:41,580
Extrinsic meaning outside, intrinsic meaning inside.

457
00:19:41,580 --> 00:19:42,580
Right.

458
00:19:42,580 --> 00:19:45,860
Functionally, extrinsic back muscles don't actually move your spine.

459
00:19:45,860 --> 00:19:46,860
They are immigrants.

460
00:19:46,860 --> 00:19:47,860
Immigrants.

461
00:19:47,860 --> 00:19:50,940
Yeah, they are muscles of the upper limb or the respiratory system that have migrated

462
00:19:50,940 --> 00:19:53,660
onto the back to find wider anchor points.

463
00:19:53,660 --> 00:19:56,940
And because they are immigrants, here is the golden rule you need to know.

464
00:19:56,940 --> 00:20:01,180
Extrinsic back muscles are innervated by the anterior rami of spinal nerves.

465
00:20:01,180 --> 00:20:02,580
Anterior meaning front.

466
00:20:02,580 --> 00:20:06,460
So nerves from the front half of the spinal cord are wrapping all the way around the rib

467
00:20:06,460 --> 00:20:09,940
cage to supply these superficial muscles on the back.

468
00:20:09,940 --> 00:20:15,020
Yes, but the intrinsic back muscles, so the deep native muscles that actually maintain

469
00:20:15,020 --> 00:20:20,740
your posture and move the vertebral column itself, they are innervated by the posterior

470
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:23,580
or dorsal rami of the spinal nerves.

471
00:20:23,580 --> 00:20:25,900
Well that is a very clean rule.

472
00:20:25,900 --> 00:20:30,460
Anterior rami for extrinsic limb movement, posterior rami for intrinsic spine movement.

473
00:20:30,460 --> 00:20:32,860
It is a clean rule and you know what that means in anatomy.

474
00:20:32,860 --> 00:20:33,860
Uh-oh.

475
00:20:33,860 --> 00:20:35,220
There's an exception that proves the rule.

476
00:20:35,220 --> 00:20:37,020
And it is the ultimate exception.

477
00:20:37,020 --> 00:20:39,540
The trapezius muscle, the one we talked about in the very beginning.

478
00:20:39,540 --> 00:20:40,540
Right.

479
00:20:40,540 --> 00:20:42,180
The massive kite-shaped muscle.

480
00:20:42,180 --> 00:20:46,220
It's an extrinsic muscle connecting the spine to the shoulder girdle.

481
00:20:46,220 --> 00:20:50,340
So by the rule, it should be innervated by an anterior ramus of a spinal nerve.

482
00:20:50,340 --> 00:20:51,340
But it isn't.

483
00:20:51,340 --> 00:20:53,180
It is innervated by a cranial nerve.

484
00:20:53,780 --> 00:20:58,300
Specifically, the accessory nerve, cranial nerve and greaei, provides its motor supply.

485
00:20:58,300 --> 00:21:02,460
A nerve from the brain case is traveling all the way down the neck and back to supply

486
00:21:02,460 --> 00:21:03,460
the trapezius.

487
00:21:03,460 --> 00:21:06,180
I mean, how did that even happen?

488
00:21:06,180 --> 00:21:08,380
It goes back to evolutionary biology again.

489
00:21:08,380 --> 00:21:13,860
So millions of years ago, the tissue that eventually became the trapezius originated

490
00:21:13,860 --> 00:21:17,860
from the brachial arches, so the gill structures, near the head and neck.

491
00:21:17,860 --> 00:21:18,860
Oh, okay.

492
00:21:18,860 --> 00:21:23,260
So as mechanisms evolved and elongated and developed complex shoulder girdles, that muscle

493
00:21:23,260 --> 00:21:27,180
tissue migrated massively down the back to stabilize the scapula.

494
00:21:27,180 --> 00:21:30,360
But it dragged its original cranial nerve down with it.

495
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:31,980
The ultimate extension cord.

496
00:21:31,980 --> 00:21:32,980
That is wild.

497
00:21:32,980 --> 00:21:33,980
Yes.

498
00:21:33,980 --> 00:21:38,540
Now, proprioception, so the sensory feedback of its position, that does come from cervical

499
00:21:38,540 --> 00:21:40,900
spinal nerves, C3 and C4.

500
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:45,400
But the actual motor command to move comes from cranial nerve XY.

501
00:21:45,400 --> 00:21:49,860
This breaks every single rule of the back, which makes it the single most tested fact

502
00:21:49,860 --> 00:21:50,860
about the trapezius.

503
00:21:50,860 --> 00:21:53,440
I am highlighting that in neon right now.

504
00:21:53,440 --> 00:21:56,240
Trapezius equals cranial nerve XY.

505
00:21:56,240 --> 00:21:58,320
What else about the trapezius do they test?

506
00:21:58,320 --> 00:22:02,760
Well, because of its massive size, its fibers run in different directions and do completely

507
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:05,080
different things to the shoulder blade or scapula.

508
00:22:05,080 --> 00:22:08,560
The superior fibers run downwards, so they elevate the scapula.

509
00:22:08,560 --> 00:22:12,760
The middle fibers run horizontally, so they retract the scapula, pulling it backward.

510
00:22:12,760 --> 00:22:17,920
And the inferior fibers run upwards, so they depress the scapula, pulling it down.

511
00:22:17,920 --> 00:22:21,400
One muscle, three completely different actions, depending on which zone is firing.

512
00:22:21,400 --> 00:22:22,760
This is super interesting.

513
00:22:22,760 --> 00:22:23,760
Okay.

514
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:29,200
So beneath the trapezius and latissimus dorsi, we have intermediate extrinsic muscles.

515
00:22:29,200 --> 00:22:32,840
Our notes list the serratus posterior superior and inferior.

516
00:22:32,840 --> 00:22:33,840
Right.

517
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:34,840
These are the respiratory immigrants.

518
00:22:34,840 --> 00:22:37,200
They are thin membranous muscles.

519
00:22:37,200 --> 00:22:40,900
The superior one elevates ribs two to five to help you inhale.

520
00:22:40,900 --> 00:22:44,900
And the inferior one depresses ribs nine to 12 to help you exhale forcefully.

521
00:22:44,900 --> 00:22:45,900
Okay.

522
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:49,860
And because they are extrinsic, they follow the rule supplied by anterior rami, specifically

523
00:22:49,860 --> 00:22:51,860
the intercostal nerves that run between the ribs.

524
00:22:51,860 --> 00:22:52,860
All right.

525
00:22:52,860 --> 00:22:55,020
So we peel all those extrinsic immigrant muscles away.

526
00:22:55,020 --> 00:22:56,020
What is underneath?

527
00:22:56,020 --> 00:22:57,660
The true intrinsic back muscles.

528
00:22:57,660 --> 00:22:58,660
Yes.

529
00:22:58,660 --> 00:23:03,940
These are enclosed by a thick layer of deep fascia known as the thoracolumbar fascia in

530
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:05,380
the lower back.

531
00:23:05,380 --> 00:23:10,700
They are native to the spine, supplied by posterior rami, and they are perfectly layered.

532
00:23:11,500 --> 00:23:15,180
The most important layer for exams is the intermediate intrinsic layer, which is known

533
00:23:15,180 --> 00:23:16,780
as the erector spinae.

534
00:23:16,780 --> 00:23:21,380
Ah, the massive columns of muscle that keep human beings standing erect.

535
00:23:21,380 --> 00:23:22,380
Exactly.

536
00:23:22,380 --> 00:23:27,100
And it is divided into three vertical columns, and this is classic matching column material

537
00:23:27,100 --> 00:23:28,240
for a test.

538
00:23:28,240 --> 00:23:33,780
From lateral to medial, so outside to inside, they are the iliofistalis, the longissimus,

539
00:23:33,780 --> 00:23:34,780
and the spinalis.

540
00:23:34,780 --> 00:23:35,780
Okay.

541
00:23:35,780 --> 00:23:39,220
So iliocostalus is on the outside attaching to the ilium and ribs.

542
00:23:39,220 --> 00:23:42,620
Spinalis is the longest, running up the middle, and spinalis is the most medial, right up

543
00:23:42,620 --> 00:23:44,900
against the spinous processes of the vertebrae.

544
00:23:44,900 --> 00:23:45,900
Perfect.

545
00:23:45,900 --> 00:23:50,980
Now, deep to the erector spinae is the deepest muscular layer of the back, the transversospinalis

546
00:23:50,980 --> 00:23:51,980
group.

547
00:23:51,980 --> 00:23:52,980
Transversospinalis.

548
00:23:52,980 --> 00:23:56,300
So breaking down the Latin, that means they run from the transverse processes, the ones

549
00:23:56,300 --> 00:23:59,740
sticking out the sides of the vertebrae, inward to the spinous processes sticking out the

550
00:23:59,740 --> 00:24:00,740
back.

551
00:24:00,740 --> 00:24:01,740
Yes.

552
00:24:01,740 --> 00:24:04,660
Their fibers run obliquely upward and medially.

553
00:24:04,660 --> 00:24:09,220
They basically occupy the gutter between these bony processes, and the way anatomists

554
00:24:09,220 --> 00:24:11,620
classify them is deeply mechanical.

555
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:16,020
They are strictly defined by exactly how many vertebral segments they span.

556
00:24:16,020 --> 00:24:17,180
Oh, numbers.

557
00:24:17,180 --> 00:24:22,020
Test writers love numbers because they're just so easy to turn into true-false statements.

558
00:24:22,020 --> 00:24:23,020
Precisely.

559
00:24:23,020 --> 00:24:25,280
So there are three members of this deep group.

560
00:24:25,280 --> 00:24:28,340
The semispinalis is the most superficial of the deep group.

561
00:24:28,340 --> 00:24:32,420
Its fibers are relatively long, spanning four to six vertebral segments.

562
00:24:32,420 --> 00:24:33,420
Okay.

563
00:24:33,420 --> 00:24:34,620
Semispinalis spans four to six.

564
00:24:34,620 --> 00:24:36,180
Deeper still is the multifidus.

565
00:24:36,180 --> 00:24:40,380
It is incredibly thick in the lower lumbar region, stabilizing the lower back.

566
00:24:40,380 --> 00:24:43,500
Its fibers are shorter, spanning two to four segments.

567
00:24:43,500 --> 00:24:44,860
Multifidus spans two to four.

568
00:24:44,860 --> 00:24:45,860
Got it.

569
00:24:45,860 --> 00:24:49,580
And the absolute deepest layer, buried right against the bone, are the rotators.

570
00:24:49,580 --> 00:24:54,340
They are best developed in the thoracic region, and their fibers span only one to two segments.

571
00:24:54,340 --> 00:24:57,660
So as you go deeper and deeper into the gutter of the spine, the muscles get shorter, they

572
00:24:57,660 --> 00:25:00,140
get tighter, and more localized.

573
00:25:00,140 --> 00:25:04,360
Semispinalis four to six, multifidus two to four, rotators one to two.

574
00:25:04,360 --> 00:25:06,400
It's almost like a countdown to the bone.

575
00:25:06,400 --> 00:25:09,920
That countdown is the exact mnemonic you need for the exam.

576
00:25:09,920 --> 00:25:14,960
Now one final structural trap on the back, way up at the top of the spine, tucked deeply

577
00:25:14,960 --> 00:25:19,720
right under the base of the skull, is a geometric space called the suboccipital triangle.

578
00:25:19,720 --> 00:25:20,720
Okay.

579
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:23,080
It's bounded by three tiny muscles.

580
00:25:23,080 --> 00:25:30,280
The rectus capitis posterior major, obliquus capitis superior, and obliquus capitis inferior.

581
00:25:30,280 --> 00:25:34,660
You know, whenever anatomists name a triangle or a space, it's usually because it holds

582
00:25:34,660 --> 00:25:37,480
something critical that an examiner really wants you to know.

583
00:25:37,480 --> 00:25:38,560
So what's inside it?

584
00:25:38,560 --> 00:25:39,880
Very important structures.

585
00:25:39,880 --> 00:25:43,880
The suboccipital triangle uniquely contains the vertebral artery and the suboccipital

586
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:44,880
nerve.

587
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:48,440
The vertebral artery, that is a major blood supply to the brain, isn't it?

588
00:25:48,440 --> 00:25:49,440
Yes, it is.

589
00:25:49,440 --> 00:25:53,760
It travels up through the cervical vertebrae, then does this drastic horizontal loop, snaking

590
00:25:53,760 --> 00:25:57,620
right through this tiny muscular triangle before finally diving up through the form

591
00:25:57,620 --> 00:25:59,120
and magnum into the skull.

592
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:00,120
Wow.

593
00:26:00,120 --> 00:26:03,120
It's highly vulnerable here, which is exactly why its location is tested.

594
00:26:03,120 --> 00:26:04,120
Okay.

595
00:26:04,120 --> 00:26:05,260
Hit me with the exam trap for the back.

596
00:26:05,260 --> 00:26:09,400
An examiner will write, the erector spinae consists of the semispinalis, multifidus,

597
00:26:09,400 --> 00:26:10,400
and rogatores.

598
00:26:10,400 --> 00:26:11,400
Ugh.

599
00:26:11,400 --> 00:26:14,160
A classic category mix-up, so that's false.

600
00:26:14,160 --> 00:26:18,080
Those three short countdown muscles are the transversospinalis group.

601
00:26:18,080 --> 00:26:23,640
The erector spinae is the massive vertical layer, so the iliocostalis, longissimus, and

602
00:26:23,640 --> 00:26:24,640
spinalis.

603
00:26:24,640 --> 00:26:25,640
Spot on.

604
00:26:25,640 --> 00:26:28,680
So to summarize the trunk, the absolute defining line is the nerve supply.

605
00:26:28,680 --> 00:26:29,680
Right.

606
00:26:29,680 --> 00:26:32,080
Intrinsic equals anterior rami.

607
00:26:32,080 --> 00:26:34,640
Intrinsic equals posterior rami.

608
00:26:34,640 --> 00:26:39,440
The trapezius breaks the rules by using a cranial nerve because of its brachial arch

609
00:26:39,440 --> 00:26:44,080
origins, and the intrinsic muscles are perfectly layered by their length.

610
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:45,080
It's amazing.

611
00:26:45,080 --> 00:26:49,280
What looks like a chaotic mess of meat on the surface is actually as highly structured

612
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:51,440
as the cables on a suspension bridge.

613
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:52,440
Yeah.

614
00:26:52,440 --> 00:26:55,200
Let's move to our final region, the abdominal wall.

615
00:26:55,200 --> 00:26:57,440
This region just fascinates me from a physics perspective.

616
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:02,520
I mean, we have a thick skull for the brain, ribs for the heart and lungs, a heavy pelvis

617
00:27:02,520 --> 00:27:07,080
for the reproductive organs, but the abdomen, it's just a soft belly holding in miles

618
00:27:07,080 --> 00:27:11,400
of intestines, the liver, the stomach, and it's all under immense pressure with absolutely

619
00:27:11,400 --> 00:27:13,200
no bones to protect it in the front.

620
00:27:13,200 --> 00:27:14,200
Right.

621
00:27:14,200 --> 00:27:16,880
How does it not just burst open when we cough or lift something heavy?

622
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:21,840
It is arguably one of the most incredible feats of biological engineering in the body.

623
00:27:21,840 --> 00:27:27,200
It all comes down to intersecting fiber directions and highly specialized fascial layers.

624
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:30,960
The abdominal wall is essentially a living, breathing corset.

625
00:27:30,960 --> 00:27:31,960
A corset.

626
00:27:31,960 --> 00:27:32,960
Yeah.

627
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:36,240
It's built like plywood, or perhaps more accurately, ballistic Kevlar.

628
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:41,200
The grain of each muscle layer runs in a completely different direction to provide maximum tensile

629
00:27:41,200 --> 00:27:44,960
strength and regulate intra-abdominal pressure.

630
00:27:44,960 --> 00:27:48,560
Let's start from the skin and go deep, and I have to ask the superficial question first.

631
00:27:48,560 --> 00:27:54,320
Everyone talks about getting a six-pack, but anatomically, what exactly creates those visible

632
00:27:54,320 --> 00:27:58,520
squares, and why doesn't the subcutaneous fat just cover it uniformly?

633
00:27:58,520 --> 00:28:02,760
Well, let's address the fat first, which is held in the superficial fascia.

634
00:28:02,760 --> 00:28:05,540
This layer has a highly testable characteristic.

635
00:28:05,540 --> 00:28:09,760
Just below the umbilicus, so your belly button, this fascia actually splits into two very

636
00:28:09,760 --> 00:28:10,760
distinct layers.

637
00:28:10,760 --> 00:28:11,760
Oh, really?

638
00:28:11,760 --> 00:28:12,760
What are the two layers?

639
00:28:12,760 --> 00:28:15,200
The superficial fatty layer is called camper's fascia.

640
00:28:15,200 --> 00:28:19,240
This is where the body stores subcutaneous fat, but deep to that, right against the muscle,

641
00:28:19,240 --> 00:28:22,400
is a tough membranous layer called scarpa's fascia.

642
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:23,400
Okay.

643
00:28:23,480 --> 00:28:24,480
Scarpa's is the fat.

644
00:28:24,480 --> 00:28:25,480
Scarpa's is the membrane.

645
00:28:25,480 --> 00:28:26,480
Correct.

646
00:28:26,480 --> 00:28:31,320
And here is the exact relationship examiner's test regarding scarpa's fascia.

647
00:28:31,320 --> 00:28:37,080
It continues inferiorly down into the perineum, but laterally, it firmly attaches to the deep

648
00:28:37,080 --> 00:28:42,400
fascia of the thigh, which is known as the fascia lata, meaning the membranous layer

649
00:28:42,400 --> 00:28:44,400
does not continue into the leg.

650
00:28:44,400 --> 00:28:46,840
It stops dead at the crease of the hip.

651
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:48,200
Why does that boundary matter?

652
00:28:48,200 --> 00:28:50,000
Does it have a clinical application?

653
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:51,000
A major one.

654
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:56,000
So imagine a patient suffers a severe straddle injury to the perineum, causing a rupture

655
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:57,840
of the urethra.

656
00:28:57,840 --> 00:29:00,320
Urine begins leaking out under pressure.

657
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:05,120
Because of where scarpa's fascia attaches, that fluid can travel freely up into the lower

658
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:09,320
abdominal wall, causing massive swelling all the way up to the belly button.

659
00:29:09,320 --> 00:29:10,320
Yikes.

660
00:29:10,320 --> 00:29:12,560
Yeah, but it cannot travel down into the thighs.

661
00:29:12,560 --> 00:29:15,560
That fascial attachment creates a hard, watertight stop.

662
00:29:15,560 --> 00:29:16,560
Wow.

663
00:29:16,560 --> 00:29:18,040
So it literally defines a fluid compartment.

664
00:29:18,040 --> 00:29:20,400
That is definitely going on the exam.

665
00:29:20,400 --> 00:29:24,800
True or false, scarpa's fascia is continuous with the deep fascia of the thigh.

666
00:29:24,800 --> 00:29:26,360
And you would answer false.

667
00:29:26,360 --> 00:29:28,360
It attaches and stops.

668
00:29:28,360 --> 00:29:29,680
No fluid gets past.

669
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:31,680
Now, let's talk about the muscles.

670
00:29:31,680 --> 00:29:33,840
The anterolateral flat muscles.

671
00:29:33,840 --> 00:29:34,840
The plywood layers.

672
00:29:34,840 --> 00:29:35,840
Right.

673
00:29:35,840 --> 00:29:38,320
There are three flat muscles on each side of the abdomen.

674
00:29:38,320 --> 00:29:39,640
Let's look at the outermost layer.

675
00:29:39,640 --> 00:29:42,540
The outermost and largest is the external oblique.

676
00:29:42,540 --> 00:29:47,240
It originates high up on the external surfaces of your lower eight ribs, and crucially, its

677
00:29:47,240 --> 00:29:49,760
fibers run inferimedially.

678
00:29:50,280 --> 00:29:53,480
So they run diagonally down and inward toward the midline.

679
00:29:53,480 --> 00:29:57,360
The classic analogy is that it's like putting your hands in your front jacket pocket.

680
00:29:57,360 --> 00:29:58,360
Exactly.

681
00:29:58,360 --> 00:29:59,800
The hands-in-pockets muscle.

682
00:29:59,800 --> 00:30:04,040
Now, as the muscle reaches the lower border of the abdomen, it's a ponderosis, so its

683
00:30:04,040 --> 00:30:08,680
flat fibrous tendon thickens and literally rolls under onto itself.

684
00:30:08,680 --> 00:30:09,680
It rolls under.

685
00:30:09,680 --> 00:30:10,680
Yeah.

686
00:30:10,680 --> 00:30:13,880
And this rolled edge forms a critical landmark, the inguinal ligament.

687
00:30:13,880 --> 00:30:16,080
Ah, the inguinal ligament.

688
00:30:16,080 --> 00:30:20,400
That spans from the anterior superior iliac spine on the outside of the pelvis down to

689
00:30:20,400 --> 00:30:22,960
the pubic tubercle near the midline.

690
00:30:22,960 --> 00:30:23,960
Yes.

691
00:30:23,960 --> 00:30:27,920
It forms the structural floor of the inguinal canal, which is a natural weak point where

692
00:30:27,920 --> 00:30:29,760
hernias often occur.

693
00:30:29,760 --> 00:30:32,800
Now, peel the external oblique away.

694
00:30:32,800 --> 00:30:34,280
Deep to it is the internal oblique.

695
00:30:34,280 --> 00:30:36,600
Its fibers run superimmediately.

696
00:30:36,600 --> 00:30:37,600
Superimmediately.

697
00:30:37,600 --> 00:30:41,720
So diagonally up and inward, like resting your fingers over your opposite chest.

698
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:42,720
Right.

699
00:30:42,720 --> 00:30:45,880
So mechanically, the fibers of the external oblique and the internal oblique cross each

700
00:30:45,880 --> 00:30:48,440
other at perfectly 90-degree right angles.

701
00:30:48,440 --> 00:30:49,440
Plywood.

702
00:30:49,440 --> 00:30:51,520
You get immense strength by crossing the grain like that.

703
00:30:51,520 --> 00:30:55,200
And the deepest of the three flat muscles is the transversus abdominis.

704
00:30:55,200 --> 00:30:59,620
As the name suggests, its fibers run horizontally directly across the abdomen.

705
00:30:59,620 --> 00:31:02,920
So we have inframedial, supermedial, and horizontal.

706
00:31:02,920 --> 00:31:05,400
It's a three-dimensional grid of tension.

707
00:31:05,400 --> 00:31:06,400
Exactly.

708
00:31:06,400 --> 00:31:12,480
Now, lower down near the pelvis, the eponerotic tendons of the internal oblique and the transversus

709
00:31:12,480 --> 00:31:17,080
abdominis actually merge together into a single fused sheet.

710
00:31:17,080 --> 00:31:19,120
This is called the conjoint tendon.

711
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:23,280
Conjoint tendon, formed only by the internal oblique and transversus abdominis.

712
00:31:23,280 --> 00:31:25,620
Does the external oblique join that party at all?

713
00:31:25,620 --> 00:31:26,840
It does not.

714
00:31:26,840 --> 00:31:30,280
The external oblique is busy forming the inguinal ligament.

715
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:35,200
The conjoint tendon attaches to the pubic crest and acts as a rigid backstop, providing

716
00:31:35,200 --> 00:31:40,960
vital structural strength behind the superficial inguinal ring to prevent herniation.

717
00:31:40,960 --> 00:31:45,680
So if an examiner asks, you know, which muscle does not contribute to the conjoined tendon,

718
00:31:45,680 --> 00:31:47,360
the answer is the external oblique.

719
00:31:47,360 --> 00:31:48,360
Right.

720
00:31:48,360 --> 00:31:49,360
Okay.

721
00:31:49,360 --> 00:31:50,360
What about the six-pack itself?

722
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:51,720
We haven't even touched the actual front of the abdomen.

723
00:31:51,720 --> 00:31:56,760
So right in the midline, we have two vertical muscles called the rectus abdominis, separated

724
00:31:56,760 --> 00:31:59,400
by a white fibrous band called the linea alba.

725
00:31:59,400 --> 00:32:01,020
The straight abdominal muscle.

726
00:32:01,020 --> 00:32:05,600
The six-pack appearance is actually not six individual muscles.

727
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:10,440
It is one long, continuous muscle belly that is physically strapped down by structures

728
00:32:10,440 --> 00:32:11,840
called tendinous intersections.

729
00:32:11,840 --> 00:32:13,280
Oh, I see.

730
00:32:13,280 --> 00:32:18,200
These are transverse fibrous bands that anchor the rectus muscle to the front of its fascial

731
00:32:18,200 --> 00:32:19,200
sheath.

732
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:20,400
But why anchor it down?

733
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:24,120
Why not just have one smooth, bulging muscle?

734
00:32:24,120 --> 00:32:28,240
If it wasn't anchored, whenever you contracted your abs to sit up, the long muscle would

735
00:32:28,240 --> 00:32:30,800
bowstring outward into a massive single lump.

736
00:32:30,800 --> 00:32:31,800
That would look weird.

737
00:32:31,800 --> 00:32:33,740
Yeah, it wouldn't be very functional.

738
00:32:33,740 --> 00:32:38,400
The tendinous intersections segment the muscle, allowing it to curve with your spine smoothly

739
00:32:38,440 --> 00:32:40,720
without bulging outward.

740
00:32:40,720 --> 00:32:44,720
They're usually located at three points, the xiphoid process at the bottom of the sternum,

741
00:32:44,720 --> 00:32:47,160
the umbilicus, and halfway between them.

742
00:32:47,160 --> 00:32:49,020
That makes perfect mechanical sense.

743
00:32:49,020 --> 00:32:51,000
But you mentioned a fascial sheath.

744
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:52,920
The rectus abdominis is wrapped up.

745
00:32:52,920 --> 00:32:53,920
Yes.

746
00:32:53,920 --> 00:32:59,300
The entire muscle is enclosed in a rigid, fibrous sleeping bag called the rectus sheath.

747
00:32:59,300 --> 00:33:05,080
This sheath is created by the flat tendons, the eponeroses, of all three flat lateral muscles

748
00:33:05,080 --> 00:33:06,080
we just talked about.

749
